{
    "id": 138,
    "name": "modisa-ocsmart-biogeochemical-daily",
    "title": "MODIS-Aqua daily biogeochemical products",
    "description": "The biogeochemical products (Chlorophyll-a, Suspended Particulate Matter, Particulate Organic Carbon, absorption by Colored Dissolved Organic Matter, and Dissolved Organic Carbon) are derived from remote sensing reflectance (Rrs, measured in sr⁻¹) retrieved from the Moderate Resolution Imaging Spectroradiometer (MODIS) onboard NASA's Aqua satellite (MODIS-Aqua) and atmospherically corrected using the Ocean Color - Simultaneous Marine and Aerosol Retrieval Tool (OC-SMART) (Fan et al., 2021). These products are generated from daily Rrs observations with a nominal spatial resolution of 1 km at nadir. For each day, multiple MODIS-Aqua swath tiles covering the study area are processed individually to produce the corresponding Rrs. The resulting Rrs tiles are then merged into a single seamless daily mosaic using the SNAP Mosaicking Tool, producing a daily Rrs composite that combines all available observations for that day. The daily Rrs composites are subsequently used as input for the retrieval of the following biogeochemical products: • Chlorophyll-a concentration (CHL, mg·m⁻³): estimated using the Combination Of Neural Network models in Estimating Chlorophyll-a over Turbid and Clear Waters (CONNECT) algorithm (Tran et al., 2025). • Suspended Particulate Matter concentration (SPM, g·m⁻³): estimated using a blend of two SPM algorithms (Salah Salah et al., 2025). • Particulate Organic Carbon (POC, mg·m⁻³): estimated by combining the algorithms proposed by Loisel et al. (2002, 2018), Tran et al. (2016), and Le et al. (2016). • Absorption by Colored Dissolved Organic Matter (aCDOM, m⁻¹): estimated from the remote sensing reflectance band centered at 443 nm using the algorithm developed by Bonelli et al. (2021). • Dissolved Organic Carbon (DOC, µmol·L⁻¹): estimated using an Artificial Neural Network (ANN) machine-learning approach (Bonelli et al., 2022).",
